  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 4', NULL, 'Breakfast → Hotel checkout → First-class bullet train to Xi’an → Premium hotel check-in Afternoon: → Big Wild Goose Pagoda area → Private sightseeing Evening: → Musical Fountain → Premium dining → Overnight at Xi’an', 2003),
  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 5', NULL, 'Breakfast Morning: → Private Terracotta Army experience → Cultural sightseeing Afternoon: → Xi’an City Wall → Muslim Quarter → Premium local experience Evening: → Fine dining → Overnight at Xi’an', 2004),
  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 6', NULL, 'Breakfast → Hotel checkout → First-class bullet train to Shanghai → Private hotel transfer → Luxury hotel check-in Evening: → Nanjing Road → The Bund → Premium Huangpu River cruise → Overnight at Shanghai', 2005),
  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 7', NULL, 'Breakfast Morning: → Private Shanghai heritage tour → Jade Buddha Temple → Yu Garden Afternoon: → Shanghai Museum → Luxury shopping → Leisure time Evening: → Rooftop / fine-dining experience → Overnight at Shanghai', 2006),
  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 8', NULL, 'Breakfast → Private Zhujiajiao Water Town excursion → Historic lanes & canals → Scenic photography Afternoon: → Return to Shanghai → Spa / luxury shopping → Leisure time Evening: → Farewell fine-dining experience → Overnight at Shanghai', 2007),
  ('beijing', 31, 'eight-nights-luxury-pattern', 'EIGHT-NIGHTS LUXURY PATTERN', 'Day 9', NULL, 'Breakfast → Leisure morning → Hotel checkout → Private luxury transfer to Shanghai Airport → Departure', 2008),
  ('beijing', 32, 'travel-agent-insights', 'TRAVEL AGENT INSIGHTS', 'What inexperienced planners often do wrong', NULL, '', 2009),
  ('beijing', 32, 'travel-agent-insights', 'TRAVEL AGENT INSIGHTS', 'What an experienced planner does differently', NULL, 'FORBIDDEN_CITY_AND_JINGSHAN + MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN_AND_HISTORIC_HUTONGS rather than maximum destination count.', 2010),
  ('beijing', 33, 'destination-decision-rules', 'DESTINATION DECISION RULES', NULL, NULL, 'IF: trip_length <= 3 nights THEN: prioritize_FORBIDDEN_CITY_AND_JINGSHAN + MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN_AND_HUTONGS Reason: Very short stays gain more value from properly experiencing core imperial sights and one Great Wall section than losing time to distant suburban moves. IF: trip_length >= 5 nights AND: traveler_interest == FIRST_TIME_BEIJING THEN: prioritize_FORBIDDEN_CITY + MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL + SUMMER_PALACE + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN + 798_ART_ZONE Reason: This gives a balanced introduction to imperial palaces, Great Wall landscapes, royal gardens, historic alleys, and contemporary art. IF: trip_length >= 7 nights AND: traveler_interest == FIRST_TIME_BEIJING THEN: prefer_CENTRAL_IMPERIAL_CORE + GREAT_WALL_AND_GUBEI_WATER_TOWN + DAY_TRIP_TO_TIANJIN_OR_XI_AN AND: use_SUBWAY_AND_HIGH_SPEED_RAIL_OR_PRIVATE_CAR Reason: Palaces, suburban water towns, alpine wall ridges, and high-speed rail connections offer distinct regional insights. IF: trip_type == HONEYMOON THEN: prioritize_COURTYARD_SIHEYUAN_HOTELS + AMAN_SUMMER_PALACE + SIMATAI_GREAT_WALL_NIGHT_VIEW AND: increase_BOUTIQUE_STAYS + ROOFTOP_DINING + COURTYARD_SPA + TEA_CEREMONY + COUPLE_EXPERIENCES Reason: Beijing''s romantic routes mix luxury courtyard stays, private Great Wall sunset picnics, lakeside cocktails, and fine dining. IF: traveler_type == FAMILY THEN: prioritize_MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL_TOBOGGAN + SUMMER_PALACE_DRAGON_BOATS + BEIJING_ZOO_PANDAS AND: include_UNIVERSAL_STUDIOS_BEIJING + SHICHAHAI_PEDAL_BOATS + DUMPLING_MAKING_WORKSHOP AND: reduce_MUSEUM_DENSITY + LONG_CONTINUOUS_TRANSFERS Reason: Beijing has exceptional family sights, including toboggan runs, giant pandas, lake pedal boats, and interactive food workshops. IF: traveler_type == LUXURY THEN: prefer_AMAN_SUMMER_PALACE + ROSEWOOD_BEIJING + PENINSULA_BEIJING AND: increase_PRIVATE_GUIDES + AFTER_HOURS_ACCESS + FINE_DINING + PRIVATE_TRANSFERS + HELICOPTER_TOURS + SPA Reason: Beijing high-end travel relies on private after-hours palace tours, masterchef duck carving, luxury MPV transfers, and heritage resort spas. IF: traveler_type == BUDGET THEN: prefer_BEIJING_SUBWAY + PUBLIC_EXPRESS_BUSES + WALKING + BUDGET_HUTONGS AND: book_FREE_MONUMENT_TICKETS_EARLY AND: balance_PAID_ATTRACTIONS_WITH_FREE_PARKS + HUTONG_WALKS + PUBLIC_GALLERIES Reason: Beijing can be accessed affordably using cheap subway fares, budget dumpling houses, free public parks, and advance online tickets. IF: traveler_interest == CULTURE_AND_HERITAGE THEN: prioritize_FORBIDDEN_CITY + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN + LAMA_TEMPLE + CONFUCIUS_ACADEMY AND: include_NATIONAL_MUSEUM + PEKING_OPERA + HUTONG_COURTYARD_ARCHITECTURE Reason: These sites showcase Beijing''s concentrated dynastic history, imperial ritual architecture, and traditional theater arts. IF: traveler_interest == ART_AND_ARCHITECTURE THEN: prefer_798_ART_ZONE + NATIONAL_CENTRE_FOR_PERFORMING_ARTS + CBD_SKYSCRAPERS + FORBIDDEN_CITY AND: include_CONTEMPORARY_GALLERIES + IMPERIAL_TIMBER_DESIGN + MODERN_SKYLINES Reason: This route contrasts ancient timber joinery with cutting-edge contemporary art and modern international architecture. IF: traveler_interest == GASTRONOMY THEN: prefer_PEKING_DUCK_DINING + COPPER_POT_HOT_POT + HUTONG_SNACK_TRAILS + IMPERIAL_COURT_CUISINE AND: include_DAJIAOZI_DUMPLING_HOUSES + ZHAJIANGMIAN_NOODLES + CRAFT_MICROBREWERIES Reason: Beijing dining spans crisp roast duck, alleyway noodle houses, imperial pastry art, and modern craft breweries. IF: traveler_interest == NATURE_AND_WILDLIFE THEN: prioritize_SUMMER_PALACE + BEIJING_BOTANICAL_GARDEN + WILD_GREAT_WALL_CORRIDORS AND: do_not_treat_MANICURED_IMPERIAL_GARDENS_AND_SUBURBAN_MOUNTAIN_RIDGES_AS_IDENTICAL Reason: Imperial parks offer manicured lakes and pavilions, while Huairou and Miyun feature rugged alpine ridges. IF: traveler_interest == SHOPPING THEN: prioritize_PANJIAYUAN_FLEA_MARKET + LIULICHANG_ANTIQUES + WANGFUJING_AND_SKP AND: include_TEAS_MALIANDAO + SILK_MARKET + LOCAL_HANDICRAFTS Reason: Panjiayuan offers antiques and crafts, Maliandao concentrates wholesale tea, and SKP/Wangfujing serve luxury fashion. IF: traveler_interest == SAUNA_AND_WELLNESS THEN: choose_COURTYARD_TCM_SPA + LUXURY_HOTEL_WELLNESS + THERMAL_HOT_SPRINGS_XIAOTANGSHAN AND: do_not_treat_TRADITIONAL_TCM_MASSAGE_AND_MODERN_HOTEL_SPAS_AS_IDENTICAL Reason: Beijing wellness ranges from imperial herbal foot baths and TCM reflexology to suburban hot spring resorts. IF: traveler_interest == PHOTOGRAPHY THEN: prioritize_JINGSHAN_PARK_OVERLOOK + FORBIDDEN_CITY_MOAT + MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL + 798_ART_ZONE AND: prioritize_GOLDEN_HOUR + BLUE_HOUR + CLEAR_AIR_DAYS Reason: These sites produce stunning visual contrasts between golden palace roofs, mountain wall ridges, and modern art installations. IF: trip_length <= 5 nights THEN: prefer_MAXIMUM_ONE_OR_TWO_BASES AND: avoid_EXCESSIVE_HOTEL_CHANGES Reason: Urban traffic, massive venue walking distances, and airport/station transit consume valuable sight hours. IF: route == CENTRAL_BEIJING_TO_GREAT_WALL THEN: prefer_PRIVATE_TRANSFER_OR_DIRECT_EXPRESS_BUS_OVER_MULTIPLE_SUBWAY_TRANSFERS Reason: Direct transfers eliminate transit confusion and save energy for climbing Great Wall stairs. IF: traveler_type == SENIOR OR: traveler_mobility == LIMITED THEN: prefer_ACCESSIBLE_HOTELS + SUBWAY_WITH_ELEVATORS + PRIVATE_CAR + CABLE_CAR_GREAT_WALL AND: reduce_EXCESSIVE_STEPS + HIGH_DOOR_THRESHOLDS + EXTREME_SUMMER_HEAT_WALKS Reason: High ancient thresholds and long walking paths cause fatigue; cable cars, elevators, and private cars ease travel. IF: traveler_uses_WHEELCHAIR THEN: prefer_VERIFIED_ACCESSIBLE_HOTELS + MODERN_SUBWAY_LINES + ACCESSIBLE_PARK_ROUTES + CABLE_CAR_GREAT_WALL AND: do_not_assume_HISTORIC_HUTONGS + ANCIENT_PAVILIONS + WILD_WALL_ARE_STEP_FREE Reason: Major modern hubs and cable-car wall sites are accessible, but historic door sills and mountain steps present physical barriers. IF: weather == EXTREME_SUMMER_HEAT AND: region == BEIJING_CENTRAL THEN: prioritize_EARLY_MORNING_PALACE_VISITS + AIR_CONDITIONED_MUSEUMS + EVENING_LAKESIDE_WALKS AND: reduce_MIDDAY_UNSHADED_PLAZA_EXPOSURE Reason: Beijing summer temps exceed 35°C (95°F); early starts and museum breaks maintain comfort. IF: weather == HEAVY_SMOG_OR_RAIN THEN: increase_NATIONAL_MUSEUM + LAMA_TEMPLE + ACROBATICS_SHOW + MALL_DINING AND: reduce_GREAT_WALL_VISIBILITY_DEPENDENT_TRIPS + OUTDOOR_PARKS Reason: Indoor museums, theater shows, covered markets, and tea houses remain excellent regardless of outdoor air or weather. IF: traveler_interest == FIRST_TIME_BEIJING THEN: prioritize_FORBIDDEN_CITY + MUTIANYU_GREAT_WALL + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN AND: add_ONE_OF_SUMMER_PALACE_LAMA_TEMPLE_OR_798_ART_ZONE AND: include_ONE_SIGNATURE_PEKING_DUCK_OR_TEA_CEREMONY_EXPERIENCE Reason: This structure provides a comprehensive introduction to Beijing''s premier heritage sights alongside authentic local culture. IF: traveler_interest == CULTURE_AND_RELAXATION_BALANCE THEN: allocate_60_TO_70_PERCENT_TO_CORE_SIGHTSEEING AND: allocate_REMAINING_TIME_TO_TEA_HOUSES + PARK_STROLLS + SPAS + HUTONG_LOUNGES + SHOPPING + FREE_TIME', 2011),
  ('beijing', 34, 'destination-scoring', 'DESTINATION SCORING', NULL, NULL, '| Factor | Score | | ------------------ | ----: | | Family | 95 | | Honeymoon | 92 | | Luxury | 94 | | Budget | 86 | | Adventure | 90 | | Culture | 96 | | Food | 95 | | Shopping | 92 | | Nightlife | 85 | | Relaxation | 89 | | Nature | 88 | | Accessibility | 82 | | Safety | 95 | | First-time visitor | 94 | | Photography | 96 |', 2012),
  ('beijing', 35, 'final-travel-agent-verdict', 'FINAL TRAVEL AGENT VERDICT', NULL, NULL, 'Best traveler profiles: 1. Honeymoon / romantic traveler (Aman Summer Palace, Courtyard Siheyuan Stays, Private Great Wall Sunsets, Shichahai Lakeside) 2. First-time Asia / China traveler 3. Culture / heritage traveler (Forbidden City, Temple of Heaven, Summer Palace, Ming Tombs, Lama Temple) 4. Imperial architecture & history traveler (Dynastic timber joinery, Central Axis, Hutongs, Great Wall brickwork) 5. Luxury / architectural design traveler (Aman Summer Palace, Rosewood, Peninsula, NCPA "Egg", CBD Skylines) 6. Photography traveler (Jingshan Park panorama, Forbidden City moat, Mutianyu ridges, 798 neon art) 7. Food / gastronomy traveler (Crisp Peking Duck, Copper-pot mutton hot pot, Zhashangmian, Imperial court cuisine) 8. Family traveler (Mutianyu Great Wall toboggan, Universal Studios Beijing, Panda House, Shichahai pedal boats) 9. Modern art & urban lifestyle traveler (798 Art Zone, Sanlitun nightlife, Wangfujing, CBD rooftop bars) 10. Budget / independent traveler (Beijing Subway, Hutong walking tours, public parks, street food stalls) Best duration: 5–7 nights 2–3 nights: Best for ONE primary focus such as Central Imperial Core + Mutianyu Great Wall express trip. 4–5 nights: Best for TWO major bases/focuses such as Central Imperial Core + Summer Palace & 798 Art Zone. 6–7 nights: Excellent for Forbidden City + Great Wall + Summer Palace + Temple of Heaven & Hutongs + Day trip to Tianjin/Gubei Water Town. 8–10 nights: Best for deep regional travel, Simatai Night Wall + Gubei Water Town, Qing East Tombs, or high-speed rail connections to Xi''an/Datong. Best season: YEAR-ROUND Autumn (Sept – Nov / Jinqiu): CRISP_AIR + GOLDEN_GINKGO_TREES + COMFORTABLE_TEMPERATURES + OPTIMAL_CLEAR_SKIES + PHOTOGRAPHY. Spring (March – May): CHERRY_BLOSSOMS + IMPERIAL_PARK_BLOOMS + MILD_WEATHER + GREAT_WALL_HIKE + OUTDOOR_HUTONG_WALKS. Summer (June – August): SUMMER_PALACE_BOATING + EVENING_LAKESIDE_NIGHTLIFE + LONGER_DAYLIGHT + WATER_TOWNS + ROOFTOP_DINING. Winter (Dec – Feb): SHICHAHAI_ICE_SKATING + SNOWY_FORBIDDEN_CITY + STEAMING_HOT_POT + FEWER_TOURIST_CROWDS. Beijing official tourism guidance treats the capital as year-round: autumn (*Jinqiu*) is world-famous for clear skies and foliage; spring for imperial park blossoms; summer for lively lake life and Great Wall hikes; and winter for snowy palace photography and traditional hot pot culture. Best hotel strategy: Choose accommodation according to city transit, atmosphere, and trip purpose. For first-time visitors: DONGCHENG_OR_XICHENG_CENTRAL_CORE (Wangfujing, Dengshikou, or Qianmen) for walking access to Imperial sights. For heritage / courtyard charm: HISTORIC_HUTONGS (Nanluoguxiang / Shichahai Siheyuan courtyard hotels) for authentic local living. For luxury & modern art: CBD / SANLITUN / AMAN_SUMMER_PALACE selectively according to fine dining, nightlife, and resort spa requirements. Avoid unnecessary hotel changes within central urban districts; traffic and subway connections serve Dongcheng and Xicheng efficiently without relocating. Best transportation: Use: Beijing transportation should primarily be treated as: BEIJING_SUBWAY_FOR_URBAN_CORE + PRIVATE_TRANSFER_OR_EXPRESS_BUS_FOR_GREAT_WALL + HIGH_SPEED_RAIL_FOR_REGIONAL_TRIPS + WALKING_AND_RICKSHAW_FOR_HISTORIC_HUTONGS Must-do for first-time visitor: Beijing should not be reduced to Great Wall quick stops alone; official tourism positions IMPERIAL_PALACES + GREAT_WALL_RIDGES + HISTORIC_HUTONGS + PEKING_DUCK_GASTRONOMY + CONTEMPORARY_ART as core capital pillars. Don''t-do: Biggest advantage: Unrivaled concentration of UNESCO World Heritage sights, millennia of imperial history, world-famous Northern gastronomy, and cutting-edge urban infrastructure. Beijing combines the world''s largest palace complex (Forbidden City), iconographic mountain fortifications (Great Wall), preserved imperial gardens (Summer Palace), and dynamic modern architecture within an efficient high-speed transit hub. Official tourism highlights the UNESCO Central Axis, rich cultural performing arts, accessible high-speed rail links, and vibrant street life, catering to diverse travel styles. Biggest disadvantage: Massive physical walking scales, strict advance-ticketing requirements, and severe seasonal climate extremes. Navigating vast imperial grounds requires significant physical stamina, summer brings intense heat while winter is dry and freezing, and strict capacity caps demand advance booking across all major heritage venues. Overall destination score: 96/100', 2013),
  ('beijing', 36, 'data-quality', 'DATA QUALITY', NULL, NULL, 'information_completeness: 98/100 last_updated: 2026-08-31 last_verified: WEB_VERIFIED verified_by: BEIJING MUNICIPAL BUREAU OF CULTURE AND TOURISM / VISIT CHINA / CHINA NATIONAL TOURIST OFFICE / BEIJING SUBWAY / OFFICIAL HERITAGE VENUES sources: IMPORTANT: Forbidden City tickets, Great Wall cable car passes, train tickets, museum entry, and restaurant reservations must NOT be treated as permanently valid. Major attractions such as FORBIDDEN_CITY + NATIONAL_MUSEUM + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN + SUMMER_PALACE have strict real-name passport registration and advance timed-entry rules; current slot availability must be verified online. Urban rail (Beijing Subway) and High-Speed Rail (CRH) are the standard efficiency choices, but real-time schedules and passport check rules should be verified on official booking platforms for actual travel dates. Beijing''s climate varies significantly by season: SPRING and AUTUMN bring crisp, comfortable days ideal for outdoor park walking and Great Wall hiking. SUMMER brings high heat (exceeding 35°C/95°F) and occasional heavy rain, requiring early morning outings and hydrated park touring. WINTER brings freezing temperatures and dry winds, requiring heavy thermal wear for Great Wall ridges and outdoor walks. Accessible tourism infrastructure is available across major modern metro lines, capital airports, and primary paved routes of major heritage parks, but historic door thresholds (*Menkan*), steep ancient stairs, and Hutong alleyways are rarely step-free. Beijing should primarily be treated as: SPRING/AUTUMN: FORBIDDEN_CITY + GREAT_WALL_HIKE + TEMPLE_OF_HEAVEN + HUTONG_WALKS + PARK_BLOOMS SUMMER: SUMMER_PALACE_BOATING + EARLY_PALACE_TOURS + EVENING_LAKESIDE + MUSEUM_VISITS + 798_ART WINTER: SNOW_PALACE_PHOTOGRAPHY + SHICHAHAI_ICE_SKATING + COPPER_HOT_POT + INDOOR_THEATER_SHOWS Visitbeijing.com.cn, TravelChinaGuide, China Railway 12306, and individual attraction reservation mini-programs should be treated as primary sources for operational information. This dataset should be refreshed regularly before production use.', 2014),
  ('shanghai', 7, 'seasonality', 'SEASONALITY', 'Spring', NULL, 'months: March April May weather: March is cool and increasingly mild, April is pleasantly warm, while May becomes warmer and more humid. Temperatures commonly range from roughly 11–21°C during spring, although frequent light rain and rapid temperature changes are possible. crowd_level: MEDIUM price_level: MEDIUM overall_score: 79–84 recommended: true reason: One of Shanghai''s strongest periods for city walking, gardens, blossoms, architecture, waterfront sightseeing, shopping, museums, photography, and nearby water-town excursions. Best for: Shanghai''s municipal government describes spring as warm, flower-filled, and the city''s best tourism season, although drizzle is common.', 2015),
  ('shanghai', 7, 'seasonality', 'SEASONALITY', 'Summer', NULL, 'months: June July August weather: Hot, very humid, and rainy. Mid-June to early July normally brings the plum-rain season, while July and August frequently experience temperatures above 35°C. Thunderstorms are common, and tropical systems can occasionally bring heavy rain and strong winds. crowd_level: MEDIUM → HIGH price_level: MEDIUM → HIGH overall_score: 63–76 recommended: true reason: Excellent for: Shanghai remains highly active in summer, but extreme humidity, heat, plum rains, thunderstorms, and occasional typhoon disruption substantially reduce outdoor sightseeing comfort.', 2016),
  ('shanghai', 7, 'seasonality', 'SEASONALITY', 'Autumn', NULL, 'months: September October November weather: September remains warm and humid, October is usually mild and increasingly comfortable, while November becomes cooler, drier, and excellent for walking. Late August through mid-September carries greater typhoon risk. crowd_level: MEDIUM → HIGH price_level: MEDIUM → HIGH overall_score: 72–89 recommended: true reason: Shanghai''s strongest overall season for comfortable city exploration, waterfront walks, architecture, parks, food, photography, shopping, and water-town excursions. Best for: November offers particularly strong weather-to-crowd value. However, National Day Golden Week around early October must be treated separately because Shanghai receives exceptionally heavy domestic tourism demand. In the 2025 eight-day National Day / Mid-Autumn holiday, Shanghai recorded approximately 25.49 million visitor trips.', 2017),
  ('shanghai', 7, 'seasonality', 'SEASONALITY', 'Winter', NULL, 'months: December January February weather: Cold, damp, and often cloudy. Typical winter temperatures are roughly 3–11°C, although cold northerly winds can make conditions feel significantly colder. Snow is uncommon and may not occur at all in some years. crowd_level: LOW → HIGH price_level: LOW → MEDIUM overall_score: 71–81 recommended: true reason: Excellent for: Outside major holidays, winter provides good crowd and accommodation-value advantages for culture, shopping, dining, and urban sightseeing. However, Lunar New Year / Spring Festival dates vary annually and can cause major transport demand, attraction crowds, and temporary price increases. Shanghai welcomed 21.67 million tourists during the nine-day 2026 Spring Festival holiday, while the wider Spring Festival transport period involved exceptionally high passenger volumes.', 2018),
  ('shanghai', 10, 'budget-strategy', 'BUDGET STRATEGY', NULL, NULL, 'Where money can be saved: Where saving money is usually NOT recommended: Travel-agent rule: In Shanghai, **METRO_ADJACENT_HOTEL + MOBILE_PAYMENT_AND_VPN + ADVANCE_TICKET_RESERVATIONS + LOCAL_DINING_AND_SKYLINE_WALKS should be evaluated together**. For short stays, prioritize **CENTRAL_METRO_BASE + BUND_AND_LUJIAZUI_SKYLINE_WALK + FRENCH_CONCESSION_AND_YU_GARDEN**. For longer stays, add **SHANGHAI_DISNEYLAND_OR_WEST_BUND + ZHUJIAJIAO_WATER_TOWN + SUZHOU_OR_HANGZHOU_HIGH_SPEED_DAY_TRIP** rather than changing inner-city hotels frequently.', 2019),
  ('shanghai', 12, 'arrival-transfer-logic', 'ARRIVAL TRANSFER LOGIC', 'Family', 'family', 'Shanghai’s primary air gateways are Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG) and Shanghai Hongqiao International Airport (SHA), alongside major high-speed rail hubs (Shanghai Railway Station, Shanghai South, and Shanghai Hongqiao Railway Station). For families arriving with young children and multi-bag luggage, pre-booked airport shuttles, official airport taxis, or private hotel minivans offer the smoothest, hassle-free transition across surface roads directly to central accommodations. When traveling between major tourist hubs or adjacent Yangtze Delta cities with kids, high-speed bullet trains (G/D trains connecting Hongqiao Railway Station to Hangzhou or Suzhou) or inter-airport express rail links offer fast, reliable, weather-proof transit equipped with spacious luggage bays.', 2020),
  ('shanghai', 12, 'arrival-transfer-logic', 'ARRIVAL TRANSFER LOGIC', 'Honeymoon', 'honeymoon', 'Opt for pre-arranged private luxury transfers (e.g., chauffeured Mercedes S-Class or V-Class MPV) directly from airport arrival terminals to luxury waterfront hotels on The Bund or leafy boutique stays in the Former French Concession. For multi-destination honeymoon itineraries, combine: PRIVATE_AIRPORT_TRANSFER + HIGH_SPEED_BULLET_TRAIN_BUSINESS_CLASS (Shanghai–Suzhou–Hangzhou–Beijing corridor) + PRIVATE_CHAUFFEUR_FOR_SUBURBAN_DISNEYLAND_AND_JIANGNAN_WATER_TOWN_LODGES.', 2021),
  ('shanghai', 12, 'arrival-transfer-logic', 'ARRIVAL TRANSFER LOGIC', 'Luxury', 'luxury', 'Utilize pre-booked private chauffeured transfers directly from major airport arrival terminals or private aviation facilities (PVG / SHA FBO VIP Terminals). For complex luxury Shanghai circuits, combine: VIP_AIRPORT_MEET_AND_GREET + PRIVATE_CHAUFFEUR_TRANSFERS + CHARTERED_HUANGPU_RIVER_YACHT_EXCURSIONS + PRIVATE_FRENCH_CONCESSION_HISTORIAN_GUIDES_AND_DISNEY_VIP_TOUR_SERVICES.', 2022),
  ('shanghai', 12, 'arrival-transfer-logic', 'ARRIVAL TRANSFER LOGIC', 'Budget', 'budget', 'Take advantage of Shanghai’s world-class, ultra-efficient public transit network directly connecting airport terminals and train stations to the metropolis: For onward suburban travel, utilize public express buses or suburban rail routes (such as Line 17 to Zhujiajiao Water Town or Line 11 to Disney Resort).', 2023),
  ('shanghai', 12, 'arrival-transfer-logic', 'ARRIVAL TRANSFER LOGIC', 'Senior', 'senior', 'Prioritize door-to-door pre-booked taxi transfers or hotel shuttles directly at terminal drop-offs to avoid extensive walking across massive multi-level transit hubs like Hongqiao or Pudong. When using intercity rail, book first-class or business-class seats, utilize station porter service (*Red Cap*), and select central accommodations near ground-level subway exits equipped with elevator and escalator access.', 2024),
  ('shanghai', 18, 'family-travel', 'FAMILY TRAVEL', NULL, NULL, 'child_friendly_score: 94 best_ages: Rules: For young children: Prefer: Avoid:', 2025),
  ('shanghai', 19, 'honeymoon-travel', 'HONEYMOON TRAVEL', NULL, NULL, 'honeymoon_score: 96 romance_score: 95 privacy_score: 92 luxury_score: 97 Recommended: Honeymoon itineraries should generally stay at: 2–3 major experiences/day. Prefer: LUXURY_WATERFRONT_HOTELS + FRENCH_CONCESSION_WALKS + TEA_CEREMONY + BUND_FINE_DINING + RIVER_SUNSET_CRUISE + FREE_TIME over: Constant multi-city flights and daily hotel changes.', 2026),
  ('shanghai', 20, 'luxury-travel', 'LUXURY TRAVEL', NULL, NULL, 'luxury_score: 97 Recommended:', 2027),
  ('shanghai', 21, 'adventure', 'ADVENTURE', NULL, NULL, 'adventure_score: 82 Shanghai offers dynamic urban and regional adventure across indoor winter sports, canal kayaking, theme park thrills, and nearby mountain trekking. Adventure experiences: Best regions: PUDONG_DISNEY_CORRIDOR + WEST_BUND_RIVERSIDE + QINGPU_WATER_TOWN_LAKES + SHESHAN_NATIONAL_FOREST + SUZHOU_HANGZHOU_RAIL_CORRIDOR', 2028),
  ('shanghai', 22, 'senior-travel', 'SENIOR TRAVEL', NULL, NULL, 'senior_suitability: 90 walking_intensity: LOW–MEDIUM for Huangpu River ferries, classical garden strolls, silk shopping, tea tastings, and Shanghai Museum tours MEDIUM–HIGH for full-length Bund promenades, Nanjing Road pedestrian street, Yu Garden stone bridges, and multi-level shopping complexes Rules: Shanghai works exceptionally well for seniors as an: HAIPAI_HERITAGE + TEA_CULTURE + MODERN_SKYLINE_VIEWS + RIVER_CRUISES + EASY_METRO_STROLLS destination.', 2029),
  ('shanghai', 23, 'accessibility', 'ACCESSIBILITY', NULL, NULL, 'wheelchair_accessibility: HIGH IN MODERN AIRPORTS, MAGLEV, HIGH-SPEED RAIL STATIONS, AND NEW METRO LINES VARIABLE TO LIMITED IN ANCIENT WATER TOWNS, HISTORIC SHIKUMEN ALLEYWAYS, AND OLDER PAVILIONS public_transport_accessibility: HIGH on modern metro lines (Lines 2, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17, 18) with step-free elevators; MODERATE on older lines (Lines 1, 3, 5) hotel_accessibility: MUST_BE_VERIFIED_PER_HOTEL attraction_accessibility: MUST_BE_VERIFIED_PER_ATTRACTION Rule: Never assume universal step-free access across centuries-old garden thresholds and historic stone bridge steps. Additional notes:', 2030),
  ('shanghai', 24, 'food', 'FOOD', NULL, NULL, 'Shanghai is particularly strong for: Food experiences: Family: Prioritize Xiaolongbao soup dumplings, pan-fried buns, scallion oil noodles, sweet & sour ribs, egg tarts, and fried rice cakes. Honeymoon: Choose romantic French Concession villa dining, private Bund skyline tasting menus, and lakeside craft cocktail pairings. Luxury: Include multi-course Shanghainese fine dining, private masterchef hairy crab feasts, and multi-sensory culinary experiences. Budget: Use local dumpling shops, pan-fried bun stalls, fast-food set meals (*Kuai Can*), and food courts in subway station basements.', 2031),
  ('shanghai', 25, 'shopping', 'SHOPPING', NULL, NULL, 'Strong categories: Luxury shopping: Prefer: Budget: Prefer: Travel-agent rule: Use WEST_NANJING_ROAD / IFC_MALL for HIGH_END_LUXURY_AND_FASHION, TIANZIFANG / M50 for ARTS_DESIGN_AND_CRAFTS, and TIANSHAN_TEA_CITY / SUPERMARKETS for TEAS_FOOD_GIFTS_AND_PASTRIES.', 2032),
  ('shanghai', 26, 'nightlife', 'NIGHTLIFE', NULL, NULL, 'nightlife_level: 92 Suitable for: Best areas: Evening activities: The Bund offers ultra-glamorous skyline lounges, the French Concession provides intimate speakeasies and jazz dens, and Xintiandi features energetic pub terraces. Honeymoon travelers should generally prefer: ROOFTOP_COCKTAILS + RIVERFRONT_NIGHT_STROOLLS + HISTORIC_JAZZ_CLUBS + ILLUMINATED_SKYLINE_VIEWS over: Rowdy late-night dance clubs. Travel-agent rule: Position Shanghai as a **FUTURISTIC_SKYLINE + WORLD_CLASS_GASTRONOMY + FRENCH_CONCESSION_SPEAKEASIES + HUANGPU_RIVER_NIGHTLIFE DESTINATION**, with modern night entertainment serving as a primary highlights pillar."', 2033),
  ('shanghai', 28, 'sample-itinerary-patterns', 'SAMPLE ITINERARY PATTERNS', '3 NIGHTS', NULL, '### Day 1 Arrival at Shanghai Pudong International Airport (PVG) or Hongqiao International Airport (SHA) → Shanghai Maglev Train / Metro Line 2 or private transfer to central hotel → Hotel check-in & transit card / mobile payment setup Afternoon: → Stroll through the leafy tree-lined streets of the Former French Concession (Wukang Road & Anfu Road) → Panoramic golden hour walk along The Bund historic promenade → Take the public Huangpu River Ferry across to Lujiazui Evening: → Welcome dinner featuring authentic Xiaolongbao & Shanghainese cuisine (Din Tai Fung, Jia Jia Tang Bao, or Old Jesse) → Rooftop drinks overlooking the neon Pudong skyline (FLAIR, Char Bar, or Vue Bar) → Hotel PVG and SHA serve as Shanghai''s major air gateways, offering the high-speed Maglev Train (PVG to Longyang Road in 8 minutes) and direct Metro Lines (Lines 2 & 10) straight into central hubs like People''s Square and East Nanjing Road. ### Day 2 Morning: Breakfast → Exploration of classical Ming-dynasty Yu Garden & Yuyuan Bazaar → Imperial architecture & rockery tour with tea tasting at Mid-Lake Pavilion (*Houxinting*) → Local street food lunch featuring pan-fried pork buns (*Shengjianbao*) and scallion oil noodles Afternoon: → Comprehensive guided tour of the world-class Shanghai Museum at People''s Square → Walk through the historic Shikumen courtyard alleyways of Xintiandi or Tianzifang → Afternoon tea break at a restored 1920s concession villa Evening: → Fine dining Shanghainese court dinner or Michelin-starred fusion meal → Evening Huangpu River Light Show Sightseeing Cruise → Hotel ### Day 3 Recommended first-time option: → Scenic morning high-speed train or private transfer to Zhujiajiao Ancient Water Town → Gondola-style boat cruise along historic canals and ancient stone bridges (Fangsheng Bridge) Afternoon: → Canal-side lunch featuring local water-town delicacies (Braised Pork Belly in bamboo leaves, river shrimp) → Exploration of ancient courtyards, quiet tea houses, and Kezhi Garden → Return transfer or high-speed rail back to central Shanghai Evening: → Fast-track high-speed elevator ascent to the Shanghai Tower Observation Deck (118th floor) → Farewell dinner along East Nanjing Road or West Nanjing Road → Night walk along the illuminated West Bund Riverside Corridor or M50 Creative Park → Hotel Optional alternative instead of Water Town Day Trip: SPA & WELLNESS: → Luxury hotel spa treatments and traditional TCM wellness at The Peninsula or Waldorf Astoria Spa → Traditional tea ceremony in Yu Garden CULTURE / HISTORY: → West Bund Art Corridor & Long Museum visit OR China Art Museum (former 2010 Expo Pavilion) → Urban Planning Exhibition Center & Jade Buddha Temple history walk FAMILY: → Shanghai Disneyland Resort theme park full day OR Shanghai Natural History Museum & Ocean Aquarium → West Bund Park riverside cycling & Huangpu river ferry rides HONEYMOON: → Day trip to Suzhou''s UNESCO Classical Gardens (Humble Administrator''s Garden) via 30-minute bullet train → Private Bund rooftop dining & luxury waterfront suite stay FOOD: → Shanghai culinary street food tour through Yunnan South Road or Wukang Market sampling soup dumplings, wontons, and crab cakes → Craft beer tasting at local microbreweries (Boxing Cat / Shanghai Brewery) For only 3 nights, prioritize: THE_BUND_AND_LUJIAZUI + YU_GARDEN_AND_FRENCH_CONCESSION + ZHUJIAJIAO_WATER_TOWN_OR_SHANGHAI_TOWER OR THE_BUND + SHANGHAI_DISNEYLAND + SUZHOU_HIGH_SPEED_DAY_TRIP rather than trying to cover outlying regional provinces. ### Day 4 Breakfast → Leisurely morning concession walk or souvenir shopping (Qipao, Jasmine/Longjing tea, silk, local pastries) → Hotel checkout → Express Maglev train, metro, or private transfer to PVG / SHA Airport → Departure', 2034),
  ('shanghai', 29, 'six-nights-honeymoon-pattern', 'SIX-NIGHTS HONEYMOON PATTERN', 'Day 1', NULL, 'Arrival at Shanghai → Private airport transfer → Romantic hotel check-in → Relax and refresh Evening: → Leisure time → The Bund waterfront → Romantic dinner → Overnight at Shanghai', 2035),
